FT                   /note="Domain:cl11399:HP Histidine phosphatase domain found
FT                   in a functionally diverse set of proteins, mostly
FT                   phosphatases; contains a His residue which is
FT                   phosphorylated during the reaction. The histidine
FT                   phosphatase superfamily is so named because catalysis
FT                   centers on a conserved His residue that is transiently
FT                   phosphorylated during the catalytic cycle. Other conserved
FT                   residues contribute to a 'phosphate pocket' and interact
FT                   with the phospho group of substrate before, during and
FT                   after its transfer to the His residue. Structure and
FT                   sequence analyses show that different families contribute
FT                   different additional residues to the 'phosphate pocket'
FT                   and, more surprisingly, differ in the position, in sequence
FT                   and in three dimensions, of a catalytically essential
FT                   acidic residue. The superfamily may be divided into two
FT                   main branches.The smaller branch 2 contains predominantly
FT                   eukaryotic proteins. The catalytic functions in members
FT                   include phytase, glucose-1-phosphatase and multiple
FT                   inositol polyphosphate phosphatase. The in vivo roles of
FT                   the mammalian acid phosphatases in branch 2 are not fully
FT                   understood, although activity against lysophosphatidic acid
FT                   and tyrosine-phosphorylated proteins has been demonstrated.
FT                   [Superfamily, Aligned:1-122, Eval:1.1e-22, score:85.0,
FT                   C-term missing]"
FT                   /note="MGA_1963"
